1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,071
How do we know a new merchant really owns the email they typed?

2
00:00:03,071 --> 00:00:07,642
Anyone can type any address, so a sign-up alone proves nothing.

3
00:00:07,642 --> 00:00:13,702
The contract adds three calls: register, verify the email, and resend the code.

4
00:00:13,702 --> 00:00:19,047
Registering saves the merchant as pending, and refuses an email that is already taken.

5
00:00:19,047 --> 00:00:25,178
A one-time code then goes to that email, from the OTP library that any module can use.

6
00:00:25,178 --> 00:00:33,523
Redis keeps only a keyed hash of the code with a configured lifetime, and one script counts the sends and stores the code in a single step.

7
00:00:33,523 --> 00:00:38,416
The email goes out through the notification service, and locally it lands in Mailpit.

8
00:00:38,416 --> 00:00:43,226
The merchant sends the code back, and only then is the email marked as verified.

9
00:00:43,226 --> 00:00:47,309
Wrong codes count against a configured limit, and too many lock the code.

10
00:00:47,309 --> 00:00:50,690
Sends are limited too, so nobody can flood an inbox.

11
00:00:50,690 --> 00:01:01,250
Every failure has its own stable code, and its message in English, Russian and Uzbek: email taken, code invalid, code expired, or too many attempts.

12
00:01:01,250 --> 00:01:05,726
Next, the merchant moves through a lifecycle, from pending to active.

